        id: "5-policy-statement",
        title: "4\\. Policy Statement",
        level: 2,
        order: 5,
        body: "4.1 A comprehensive patient assessment shall be completed by a qualified registered nurse at the Start of Care visit and at all subsequent applicable OASIS time points. The comprehensive assessment shall begin at the time of the first billable visit — the SOC visit — and shall be completed at the patient's home.\n4.2 Only a registered nurse currently licensed by the California Board of Registered Nursing and meeting the assessment competency requirements of this policy may complete a comprehensive assessment for home health patients. Licensed vocational nurses, therapists, and other clinical disciplines shall not complete comprehensive assessments or OASIS assessments independently, regardless of the clinical services they provide.\n4.3 The comprehensive assessment shall be completed within 5 calendar days following the start of the care episode — meaning the SOC assessment shall be completed at or within 5 calendar days of the first billable visit per 42 CFR § 484.55(a)(1). The 5-day window applies to the completion and locking of the assessment in the EHR, not merely to the collection of data during the visit.\n4.4 The comprehensive assessment shall include all domains specified in Section 6.2 and shall incorporate all applicable OASIS data elements as defined in the current CMS OASIS-E2 guidance. The assessment shall be individualized and patient-specific — template-driven assessments that do not reflect the patient's actual clinical presentation at the time of the assessment are not compliant with 42 CFR § 484.55.\n4.5 The comprehensive assessment shall include a determination of homebound status per CL-CA-005, clearly documented with patient-specific functional limitations that support the homebound determination.\n4.6 The comprehensive assessment shall include a determination of the patient's need for skilled services — specifically identifying which skilled disciplines are required and why they are medically necessary for the treatment of this patient's conditions.\n4.7 The comprehensive assessment findings shall form the direct basis of the plan of care. Every element of the plan of care — diagnoses, services, frequency, goals, medications, treatments, and safety measures — shall be traceable to findings documented in the comprehensive assessment.\n4.8 The assigned RN shall incorporate or coordinate with the findings of all discipline-specific evaluations (PT, OT, SLP, MSW) conducted at or near the SOC into the unified plan of care, ensuring that the plan is a truly interdisciplinary document rather than a series of parallel, disconnected discipline plans.\n4.9 All OASIS items shall be completed accurately, using the current CMS OASIS-E2 Guidance Manual for all response selection decisions. The RN completing the assessment is solely responsible for the accuracy of the OASIS coding — OASIS responses shall not be altered by non-clinical staff, revenue cycle personnel, or billing coders.\n4.10 A comprehensive re-assessment shall be conducted at each OASIS time point (ROC, RECERT, FU as applicable, TRN, DC). The re-assessment shall reflect the patient's current status at the time of the re-assessment — not the status at the prior assessment or the status at an earlier time point. Each OASIS assessment is a contemporaneous point-in-time assessment of the patient's current condition per CL-OA-009.\n\n---",
