        title: "Non-Recertification — When Recertification Is Not Appropriate",
        level: 3,
        order: 11,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.4.1 | Assigned RN | At every recertification assessment, evaluate the patient's continued eligibility using the three eligibility questions in Section 2 (homebound? skilled need? clinically appropriate plan?). If any question cannot be answered affirmatively with documented clinical evidence, do not proceed with recertification. | During the recertification assessment. |\n| 6.4.2 | Assigned RN | If the patient no longer meets recertification criteria, document the clinical findings supporting the non-recertification determination: the specific evidence that the patient no longer requires skilled services, is no longer homebound, or whose continued treatment would not result in meaningful clinical progress. | Within 24 hours of the recertification assessment. |\n| 6.4.3 | Assigned RN | Notify the physician within 24 hours of the non-recertification determination, providing the clinical summary and requesting a physician discharge order. Initiate discharge planning per CL-CP-006. | Within 24 hours. |\n| 6.4.4 | Assigned RN | Do not schedule or deliver skilled visits after the expiration of the current certification period if the patient is not being recertified. Services delivered after the certification period expiration without a new physician-signed recertification are not billable under Medicare. | Immediately upon non-recertification determination. |\n\n---",
