        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.4.1 | Clinician at the Patient's Home | If a clinician encounters an unsafe situation in the patient's home — including threats, violence, aggressive behavior, criminal activity, or unresolvable environmental hazards — the clinician shall: (a) exit the situation immediately if there is an imminent threat to safety; (b) call 911 if there is an imminent physical threat to anyone; (c) notify the Director of Nursing within 2 hours of the safety event. | Exit immediately if safety is at risk; Director of Nursing notification within 2 hours. |\n| 6.4.2 | Director of Nursing | Conduct a Safety Risk Assessment within 48 hours of the reported safety event. Assess whether: (a) the safety concern can be resolved through education, alternative scheduling, or care plan modification; (b) staff safety cannot be assured and continuation of visits is not appropriate. | Within 48 hours of the reported event. |\n| 6.4.3 | Director of Nursing / Administrator | If the Director of Nursing determines that the safety risk is unresolvable, initiate the discharge process. Notify the attending physician within 24 hours. Provide the patient with at least 7 calendar days' advance written notice of discharge (when it is safe to do so), along with information about alternative home health agencies and community resources, to allow the patient sufficient time to arrange alternative care. | Physician notification within 24 hours; patient notification as soon as it is safe to do so; minimum 7 calendar days advance written notice. |\n| 6.4.4 | Director of Nursing | Document the full safety event, the safety risk assessment, the physician notification, the patient notification, and all actions taken in the patient's clinical record and in the agency's incident reporting system per RM-ER-002. | Within 24 hours of each event and action. |\n\n---",
