        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.1.1 | Assigned RN / Any Clinician | When a patient requires transfer to an inpatient facility — whether due to clinical deterioration, emergent medical need, physician order, or patient/caregiver request — immediately notify the attending physician (if the physician has not already ordered the transfer) and the Director of Nursing. If the transfer is emergent, call 911 first, then notify the physician and Director of Nursing. | Immediately upon identification of transfer need; physician notification within 1 hour for non-emergent transfers. |\n| 6.1.2 | Assigned RN | Prepare the Clinical Transfer Summary (Appendix A) including all elements defined in Section 4.2. The summary shall be generated from the patient's current clinical record and shall reflect the patient's status as of the transfer date. | Before or at the time of transfer; no later than 24 hours after transfer for emergent transfers where pre-transfer summary was not possible. |\n| 6.1.3 | Assigned RN | Conduct a warm handoff with the receiving facility by contacting the receiving nurse, admissions coordinator, or emergency department clinician by telephone. Communicate: (a) the patient's name and identifying information; (b) the reason for transfer; (c) the patient's current clinical status; (d) current medications including recent changes; (e) advance directive status; (f) any transmission-based precaution status; (g) allergies. Document the warm handoff including the date, time, name of the receiving clinician, and information communicated. | At the time of transfer or as soon as the receiving facility is identified. |\n| 6.1.4 | Assigned RN / Medical Records | Transmit the Clinical Transfer Summary to the receiving facility by fax, secure electronic transmission, or hand delivery (if the patient is being transported with agency staff present). Document the method and date of transmission. | At the time of transfer; confirmed receipt documented within 24 hours. |\n| 6.1.5 | Assigned RN | Notify all other disciplines involved in the patient's care that the patient has been transferred. Document the notifications. | Within 24 hours of transfer. |\n| 6.1.6 | Assigned RN | Notify the patient's emergency contact and caregiver of the transfer, the receiving facility, and the reason for transfer. Document the notification. | Within 24 hours of transfer; at the time of transfer for planned transfers. |\n| 6.1.7 | Assigned RN | Complete the transfer OASIS assessment per CMS requirements. The transfer OASIS shall document the patient's clinical and functional status at the time of transfer, not at the time of the last visit. If the clinician was not present at the time of transfer, the OASIS shall be based on the best available clinical information from the most recent visit and any information obtained from the patient, caregiver, or receiving facility. | On the date of transfer or within CMS-defined completion timeframe; transmitted per CL-CA-003. |\n| 6.1.8 | Revenue Cycle | Assess the billing implications of the transfer — determine whether the episode is complete, whether a final claim can be submitted, and whether the transfer affects PDGM payment grouping. Coordinate with the Director of Nursing to ensure all required documentation is on file before claim submission. | Within 5 business days of transfer. |\n\n---",
