        id: "10-specific-time-point-management-high-risk-scenarios",
        title: "Specific Time Point Management — High-Risk Scenarios",
        level: 3,
        order: 10,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.3.1 | Clinical Coordinator | For RECERT assessments specifically: generate the Recertification Due Report per CL-CP-008 no later than Day 40 of each certification period. The report shall trigger the scheduling of the RECERT visit within the Day 56–60 window and the assignment of the OASIS completion deadline. If the RECERT visit is not yet scheduled by Day 50, the Director of Nursing shall be immediately notified. | Report generated at Day 40; escalation if not scheduled by Day 50. |\n| 6.3.2 | Director of Nursing | If a patient is hospitalized during the Day 56–60 RECERT window, the RECERT assessment cannot be conducted during the inpatient stay. Assess whether: (a) the patient will be discharged from the hospital before Day 60 — if so, schedule the RECERT visit immediately upon discharge; (b) the certification period will expire before the patient is discharged — if so, the patient's episode will end at Day 60 and a new SOC will be required upon discharge. Document the clinical rationale for the timing decision. Consult the Compliance Officer if billing implications are uncertain. | Within 24 hours of learning of the hospitalization during the RECERT window. |\n| 6.3.3 | Assigned RN | For TRN (Transfer) assessments: if the clinician was not present at the time of transfer (emergency transport by caregiver without prior notice), complete the TRN assessment as soon as the agency is notified of the transfer — using the patient's clinical status as documented in the most recent visit note and any information available from the patient, caregiver, or receiving facility. Clearly document in the assessment narrative that the assessment is being completed retrospectively and the sources used. | Within the regulatory deadline from the transfer date; as soon as the agency is notified. |\n| 6.3.4 | Assigned RN | For FU/SCIC assessments: when a significant change in condition is identified — through a visit, a telephone contact, or a caregiver report — notify the Director of Nursing within 24 hours and initiate the SCIC assessment process. The SCIC assessment must capture the patient's status at the time of the significant change — document the date of the significant change separately from the date of the assessment, per CMS OASIS guidance. | SCIC identification: within 24 hours of identification; assessment: per the operational target and regulatory deadline above. |\n\n---",
        scormChunkHint: "lesson",
