        id: "5-policy-statement",
        title: "4\\. Policy Statement",
        level: 2,
        order: 5,
        body: "4.1 The CMS OASIS-E2 Guidance Manual — the most current version, as published by CMS — is the sole authoritative source for all OASIS item-level coding decisions at Care Indeed Home Health Care, Inc. No other source has equivalent authority.\n4.2 When an assessor has a question about the correct coding of any OASIS item for a specific patient situation, the correct process is: first, consult the current CMS OASIS-E2 Guidance Manual for the specific item; second, review the item-specific FAQ or Q&A materials published by CMS or the relevant state OASIS contractor; third, consult the Director of Nursing or OASIS Quality Reviewer for clinical adjudication; fourth, if the guidance remains unclear, submit the question to the CMS OASIS contractor or the state OASIS coordinator through the official Q&A submission process. At no point in this process shall the assessor create an agency-specific interpretation that is not grounded in and consistent with CMS guidance.\n4.3 The agency shall maintain a current, accessible copy of the CMS OASIS-E2 Guidance Manual in the EHR reference library and shall update it within 30 calendar days of any CMS revision to the Guidance Manual. Assessors shall access the Guidance Manual from the agency's EHR reference library — not from memory, from prior-version documents, or from third-party materials that have not been verified for consistency with the current Guidance Manual.\n4.4 The following practices are explicitly prohibited at Care Indeed Home Health Care, Inc.:\n(a) Using a prior version of the OASIS Guidance Manual as the basis for any coding decision after a new version has been published and adopted.\n(b) Applying an internally developed \"cheat sheet,\" \"quick reference guide,\" or coding shortcut that selects OASIS response options through a decision pathway not grounded in the current CMS Guidance Manual. All quick reference materials used in OASIS coding must be verified against the current Guidance Manual and must explicitly note that they are supplements to — not replacements of — the Guidance Manual.\n(c) Coding OASIS items in a systematic manner that departs from the Guidance Manual's response selection rules — including but not limited to: coding GG functional items based solely on patient or caregiver report without observing the patient perform the activity; coding wound items without undressing dressings and directly observing wounds; coding behavioral health items without administering the required standardized tools in validated protocols; or reverse-staging healing pressure injuries.\n(d) Accepting OASIS coding instructions from revenue cycle staff, billing consultants, or management personnel that deviate from the CMS Guidance Manual's response selection rules, for any reason — including payment optimization.\n(e) Retaining in the agency's reference library any OASIS coding guidance, training material, or reference tool that conflicts with the current version of the CMS OASIS-E2 Guidance Manual, without clearly marking the conflicting material as \"SUPERSEDED — DO NOT USE.\"\n4.5 When the CMS Guidance Manual provides an unclear or ambiguous response to a specific clinical scenario — a situation that genuinely exists for some items — the assessor shall use the clinical reasoning documentation process per CL-OA-012 to document the specific ambiguity, the interpretive approach applied, and the clinical basis for the selected response. This documentation does not authorize deviation from CMS guidance — it documents the assessor's good-faith application of judgment where the guidance itself is ambiguous.\n4.6 The agency shall track CMS OASIS guidance updates through the CMS website, CMS OASIS listservs, and state OASIS contractor communications, and shall disseminate updates to all authorized assessors within 14 calendar days of any change to the Guidance Manual.\n\n---",
