        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.2.1 | Assessing RN | For every GG functional item applicable to the SOC OASIS, ask the patient to perform the activity during the assessment visit. Observe the performance. Code from the observation. If the patient's performance demonstrates greater independence than the discharge documentation suggested, code the greater independence. If the patient's performance demonstrates greater dependence than the discharge documentation suggested, code the greater dependence. The direct observation governs in both directions. | During the assessment visit. |\n| 6.2.2 | Assessing RN | For clinical severity and symptom items — respiratory status, cardiac status, pain, nutritional status, bladder and bowel function — conduct the physical examination and patient interview that generates Level 1 or 2 evidence for the current time point. Do not assume clinical stability from the discharge documentation; assess current status directly. | During the assessment visit. |\n| 6.2.3 | Assessing RN | For behavioral health items — administer the BIMS and PHQ-2/PHQ-9 during the current assessment visit, regardless of what behavioral health assessments the hospital may have conducted. The point-in-time principle applies to standardized tools as well: a BIMS score from the hospital reflects the patient's cognitive status at a hospital time point. The SOC BIMS reflects the patient's cognitive status in the home environment at the SOC time point. These are not interchangeable. | During the assessment visit. |\n| 6.2.4 | Assessing RN | Conduct a complete skin and wound assessment at the SOC visit by undressing all dressings. Do not code wound items from the hospital's wound documentation without direct observation. New wounds may have developed since discharge; prior wounds may have changed in stage or character; wounds documented in the hospital record may not be present at the SOC. | During the assessment visit. |\n| 6.2.5 | Assessing RN | If a patient's condition makes direct observation of a specific functional activity unsafe at the current SOC visit — for example, the patient's fall risk is so high that attempting ambulation would be dangerous — document the clinical reason observation was not performed and apply the substitute evidence protocol per CL-OA-006 Section 6.1 (Level 5 evidence with clinical justification). Do not default to the discharge documentation without documenting the reason direct observation was contraindicated. | At the time of the assessment; documented within 24 hours. |\n\n---",
