        id: "19-clinical-reasoning-documentation-structure",
        title: "6\\. Clinical Reasoning Documentation Structure",
        level: 2,
        order: 19,
        body: "The following structured format shall be used for all clinical reasoning documentation:\n[Item Code] — Clinical Reasoning Entry:\n\"[OASIS item code and name]: Clinical judgment required for this coding decision. [Triggering condition]: [Describe the specific triggering condition]. Available evidence: [Describe the specific clinical evidence available — source, content, and evidence hierarchy level per CL-OA-006]. Alternative responses considered: [Identify the response options that were candidates]. Coding reasoning: [Explain why the selected response most accurately represents the patient's status per the applicable OASIS item definition]. Selected response: [State the response selected and confirm it is consistent with the CMS Guidance Manual definition for that response option].\"\nExample — Condition 1 (Variable Performance):\n\"GG0170B (Walking 10 Feet) — Clinical Reasoning Entry: Clinical judgment required. Triggering condition: Variable performance across the 7-day look-back. Available evidence (Level 4 — patient self-report corroborated by caregiver): Patient reports that on most days she walks from bedroom to bathroom (approximately 15 feet) without physical assistance, holding the wall for support. However, on two days this week (per caregiver report), patient required hand-holding assistance from the caregiver at the bathroom threshold due to increased dizziness following medication change on 7/3. Direct observation today (7/5): Patient walked 15 feet to the bathroom without physical contact assistance; required verbal cueing at the threshold. Alternative responses considered: 04 (Supervision or Touching Assistance — reflects typical performance most days and direct observation today) vs. 03 (Partial/Moderate Assistance — reflects the two days with hand-holding). Coding reasoning: Per CMS OASIS guidance for GG items, code reflects the patient's usual performance — the level that most commonly describes the patient. Patient performs independently or with supervision/touching on 5 of 7 days. The 2-day variance was associated with a new medication that has now been dose-adjusted. Typical performance is supervision/touching assistance. Selected response: 04 — Supervision or Touching Assistance.\"\n\n---",
