        id: "15-what-surveyors-and-auditors-will-look-for",
        title: "What Surveyors and Auditors Will Look For",
        level: 3,
        order: 15,
        body: "CMS surveyors and MAC ADR auditors conducting assessments of OASIS data quality will specifically examine:\n1. Internal consistency of the OASIS assessment. Surveyors are trained to identify logical inconsistencies within the OASIS data set — a patient coded as fully independent in all GG mobility items but with a documented pressure injury, for example, or a patient coded as having severe cognitive impairment but coded as independently managing a complex medication regimen. These internal contradictions suggest that the assessor was not applying a coherent clinical picture across the assessment.\n2. Consistency between the OASIS and the clinical narrative. Auditors will compare the OASIS item responses against the assessment narrative documentation. The narrative is expected to provide the clinical basis for the OASIS response — when the narrative description of a patient's functional status during the SOC visit is inconsistent with the GG item response, the auditor must determine which is accurate. When neither the narrative nor the OASIS clearly establishes the clinical basis, both are treated as inadequately substantiated.\n3. Consistency between the OASIS and the physician documentation. Surveyors will compare the OASIS clinical grouping diagnoses against the physician's current orders and the discharge summary diagnoses. A primary OASIS diagnosis that does not appear in any physician documentation is a documentation integrity concern.\n4. Evidence of a systematic verification process. Surveyors are increasingly looking for agencies to demonstrate not just accurate OASIS data, but a process that systematically produces accurate OASIS data. An agency with a documented cross-document verification requirement — and evidence that it is being followed — demonstrates a governance structure that sustains accuracy over time, not just in individual assessments.\n\n---",
