        id: "9-physician-notification-for-identified-issues-m2003",
        title: "Physician Notification for Identified Issues (M2003)",
        level: 3,
        order: 9,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.2.1 | Assessing RN | When a potential clinically significant medication issue is identified during the drug regimen review, contact the patient's attending physician within one calendar day of the drug regimen review date. \"One calendar day\" means: if the drug regimen review is completed on July 5, physician contact must occur no later than July 6. Document the date and time of contact. | Within one calendar day of the drug regimen review. |\n| 6.2.2 | Assessing RN | Document the physician contact including: the specific issue communicated; the physician's response; any orders received; and any clinical guidance provided. This documentation must be present in the clinical record before M2003 is finalized. | Within 24 hours of the physician contact. |\n| 6.2.3 | Assessing RN | Code M2003 based on whether physician contact occurred within one calendar day when M2001 = 1 (problems found). M2003 is a process measure coded on timing: if physician was contacted within one calendar day, M2003 = 0 (Yes); if not contacted within one calendar day, M2003 = 1 (No). The reason for non-contact must be documented in the clinical record. | After physician contact status is confirmed. |\n| 6.2.4 | Assessing RN | If the drug regimen review finds no potential clinically significant issues (M2001 = 0), M2003 is coded as \"NA\" — physician contact is not required when no issues are identified. Do not conflate M2001 = 0 (no issues found) with a failure to conduct the drug regimen review. | At the time of M2003 coding. |\n\n---",
        scormChunkHint: "lesson",
