        title: "4\\. Policy Statement",
        level: 2,
        order: 5,
        body: "4.1 OT services shall be provided only by occupational therapists licensed by the California Board of Occupational Therapy (CBOT), or by certified occupational therapy assistants (COTAs) working under OT supervision in accordance with California BPC § 2570 et seq.\n4.2 All OT services shall be authorized by a physician order and reflected in the physician-approved plan of care per CL-CP-001 and CL-CP-003.\n4.3 The OT shall conduct an initial evaluation within 48 hours of receiving the patient referral, including: (a) review of the patient's medical history, current diagnoses, and relevant surgical history; (b) assessment of ADL and IADL performance using standardized functional assessment tools; (c) upper extremity assessment including strength, range of motion, sensation, coordination, and fine motor skills; (d) cognitive-perceptual assessment as indicated; (e) home environment assessment for adaptive equipment needs, home modifications, and safety concerns; (f) identification of patient-specific, measurable, time-bound functional goals; (g) a recommended treatment plan including visit frequency, duration, and discharge criteria.\n4.4 COTAs shall not independently conduct initial evaluations, establish goals, develop or modify the plan of care, or determine discharge. COTAs shall deliver treatment interventions under the direction of the supervising OT, with documented supervisory review at minimum every 30 calendar days or every 6th COTA visit, whichever occurs first, or per CBOT requirements if more stringent.\n4.5 All OT visit documentation shall be completed within 24 hours of the visit and shall demonstrate the skilled nature of the service, the patient's response to treatment, and measurable progress toward functional goals.\n4.6 The OT shall conduct a re-evaluation at each recertification period, or more frequently when warranted by a significant change in the patient's condition, and shall update goals and the treatment plan.\n4.7 Discharge from OT shall occur when: (a) the patient has met all OT-specific goals; (b) the patient has plateaued and further skilled OT services will not result in meaningful functional improvement; (c) the physician orders discontinuation; or (d) the patient declines further services. The OT shall document a discharge summary including final functional status, goals achieved, and recommendations for ongoing functional maintenance.\n4.8 When OT is the continuing qualifying service (the initial qualifying service has been discharged), the OT shall ensure that homebound status and ongoing eligibility documentation is maintained per CL-CA-005.\n\n---",
