        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.1.1 | Assigned RN | At the SOC visit, perform a complete skin assessment to identify all wounds — do not rely solely on the referral documentation for wound identification. Undress all dressings. Assess each wound using all elements in Section 4.3. | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.1.2 | Assigned RN | Classify and stage each wound. For pressure injuries, use the NPIAP staging system exclusively. Do not reverse-stage healing pressure injuries. For non-pressure wounds, classify by etiology (surgical, diabetic, venous, arterial, traumatic). | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.1.3 | Assigned RN | Measure each wound (L x W x D) using a disposable wound measurement tool (ruler). Note any undermining or tunneling using clock-face notation (e.g., \"tunneling at 3 o'clock, 2 cm\"). | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.1.4 | Assigned RN | Photograph each wound with patient consent per Section 4.4. | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.1.5 | Assigned RN | Document the wound assessment using the Wound Assessment Documentation Template (Appendix A). Ensure that the documentation supports accurate completion of OASIS items M1311 through M1350. | Within 24 hours of the SOC visit. |\n| 6.1.6 | Assigned RN | Develop a wound-specific care plan including: treatment goals (e.g., wound closure, infection prevention, reduction in wound size by a defined percentage within a defined timeframe); treatment interventions; dressing change frequency; and patient/caregiver wound care education. | Within 24 hours of the SOC visit. |\n\n---",
