        level: 2,
        order: 5,
        body: "4.1 Standard Precautions shall be followed by all agency staff at every patient encounter. Standard Precautions are the minimum infection prevention practices that apply to all patient care, regardless of suspected or confirmed infection status, and include: (a) hand hygiene; (b) use of personal protective equipment (PPE) based on anticipated exposure; (c) respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette; (d) safe injection practices; (e) safe handling of potentially contaminated equipment or surfaces; (f) sharps safety and disposal.\n4.2 Hand hygiene shall be performed: (a) immediately before direct patient contact; (b) before donning gloves for an aseptic procedure; (c) after removal of gloves; (d) after contact with body fluids, non-intact skin, or wound dressings; (e) after contact with the patient's immediate environment; (f) between different care activities on the same patient when moving from a contaminated to a clean body site. Alcohol-based hand rub (ABHR) with ≥60% alcohol is the preferred method when hands are not visibly soiled. Soap and water shall be used when hands are visibly soiled, after caring for patients with Clostridioides difficile or norovirus, and when ABHR is not available.\n4.3 Transmission-Based Precautions shall be implemented in addition to Standard Precautions when caring for patients with known or suspected communicable diseases: (a) Contact Precautions — for MRSA, VRE, C. difficile, scabies, and other contact-transmitted organisms; (b) Droplet Precautions — for influenza, pertussis, and respiratory viruses transmitted by large respiratory droplets; (c) Airborne Precautions — for tuberculosis, measles, varicella, and other airborne-transmitted organisms. The specific precautions for each condition shall follow current CDC guidelines.\n4.4 PPE shall be available to all staff and used according to the anticipated exposure: (a) gloves for contact with blood, body fluids, non-intact skin, mucous membranes, and contaminated surfaces; (b) gowns for procedures likely to generate splashes or when caring for patients on contact precautions; (c) masks and eye protection for procedures that may generate splashes or sprays; (d) N95 respirators for airborne precautions (all staff on airborne precaution assignment must be fit-tested per OSHA requirements).\n4.5 All sharps (needles, lancets, scalpels) shall be disposed of immediately after use in a puncture-resistant sharps container. Sharps containers shall be provided to patients receiving injectable medications at home and shall be disposed of per California Health and Safety Code requirements.\n4.6 All biohazardous waste generated during home visits shall be managed per OSHA Bloodborne Pathogen Standard (29 CFR § 1910.1030) and RM-PS-002. Clinical waste shall be contained in appropriate biohazard bags, transported safely, and disposed of through the agency's contracted biohazardous waste disposal service.\n4.7 The agency shall maintain an infection surveillance program per QA-SM-002 that includes: (a) tracking patient infections (urinary tract infections, wound infections, respiratory infections, CLABSI); (b) trending infection data to identify patterns; (c) reporting notifiable communicable diseases to the California Department of Public Health within required timeframes; (d) reporting infection data to the QAPI program for analysis and performance improvement.\n4.8 All staff shall receive initial and annual infection prevention training per HR-TD-001, including: standard precautions, hand hygiene, PPE donning and doffing, sharps safety, bloodborne pathogen exposure procedures, and transmission-based precautions.\n4.9 Exposure incidents (needlestick injuries, blood/body fluid exposures) shall be managed per OSHA post-exposure procedures: (a) immediate wound care; (b) reporting to the Director of Nursing within 2 hours; (c) medical evaluation within 2 hours per the agency's occupational health protocol; (d) incident report per RM-ER-002.\n\n---",
