        id: "6-definitions",
        title: "5\\. Definitions",
        level: 2,
        order: 6,
        body: "| Term | Definition |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| Congestive Heart Failure (CHF) | A chronic condition in which the heart does not pump blood as efficiently as it should, leading to fluid accumulation in the lungs, abdomen, and extremities. |\n| NYHA Functional Classification | The New York Heart Association classification system for heart failure severity: Class I (no limitation), Class II (slight limitation), Class III (marked limitation), Class IV (symptoms at rest). |\n| Dry Weight | The patient's baseline weight when fluid status is optimized — typically the weight at which the patient has no or minimal fluid retention. Established with physician input. |\n| Decompensation | A worsening of heart failure symptoms typically indicated by fluid overload (weight gain, edema, dyspnea), representing a clinical deterioration that often precedes hospitalization. |\n| Zone System | A patient education framework using Green/Yellow/Red color-coded zones to help patients self-assess their cardiac status and determine appropriate responses. |\n| Orthostatic Hypotension | A drop in systolic blood pressure of ≥20 mmHg or diastolic blood pressure of ≥10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing from a sitting or lying position, which increases fall risk and may indicate over-diuresis or medication adverse effect. |\n| Jugular Venous Distention (JVD) | Visible distention of the jugular veins when the patient is positioned at a 45-degree angle, indicating elevated central venous pressure and right-sided heart failure. |\n\n---",
