        title: "Pediatric SOC Assessment",
        level: 3,
        order: 9,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.2.1 | Assigned RN | Before the SOC visit, review all available referral information: birth history (for infants and young children), NICU history (if applicable), developmental history, immunization records, prior therapy evaluations, school or early intervention program records (for school-age and preschool children), and the current physician's medical summary. | Before the SOC visit. |\n| 6.2.2 | Assigned RN | At the SOC visit, obtain vital signs using age-appropriate technique and reference ranges: (a) Neonates and infants — respiratory rate and heart rate by auscultation or observation for a full 60 seconds; temperature by axillary route (preferred) or temporal artery; blood pressure using age-appropriate cuff; weight measured to the nearest gram (infants) or nearest 0.1 kg (older children); head circumference for children under 2 years; (b) Toddlers through adolescents — blood pressure with age-appropriate cuff size; heart rate and respiratory rate by auscultation; temperature by axillary, tympanic, or oral route appropriate to age; weight to nearest 0.5 kg; height for growth charting. Document all vital signs with age-appropriate normal range notation. | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.2.3 | Assigned RN | Conduct a systems-based physical assessment adapted to the patient's age, development, and primary diagnosis, including: (a) Neurological — level of consciousness (age-appropriate alertness, responsiveness, social engagement), tone (hypertonia, hypotonia), symmetry of movement, seizure history and current seizure activity; (b) Respiratory — breath sounds, respiratory pattern, work of breathing, oxygen saturation, tracheostomy or ventilator status (if applicable); (c) Cardiovascular — heart sounds, perfusion, capillary refill, peripheral pulses; (d) Gastrointestinal — feeding method (oral, nasogastric, gastrostomy tube), intake, output, weight trends, constipation or diarrhea; (e) Musculoskeletal — joint range of motion, muscle strength (age-appropriate assessment), contracture risk, positioning equipment needs; (f) Skin — diaper area assessment for infants, skin integrity for immobile patients, stoma sites, central line entry sites, cast or brace sites; (g) Developmental — current developmental milestone achievement compared to age norms; identification of any developmental concerns. | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.2.4 | Assigned RN | Conduct a developmental surveillance assessment using a validated screening tool appropriate to the patient's age. For children aged 0–5 years, use the Ages and Stages Questionnaire (ASQ-3) or equivalent. For children aged 5–17 years, use clinical milestone review and the Pediatric Symptom Checklist (PSC) or equivalent. Document the tool used and the results. If developmental concerns are identified, recommend referral to the physician for formal developmental evaluation. | At the SOC visit; findings documented within 24 hours. |\n| 6.2.5 | Assigned RN | Conduct a caregiver assessment including: (a) the caregiver's relationship to the patient (parent, guardian, foster parent, other); (b) the caregiver's understanding of the child's diagnosis and care requirements; (c) the caregiver's demonstrated competency in current home care techniques (medication administration, tube feeding, tracheostomy care, wound care — depending on the patient's needs); (d) the caregiver's learning readiness and preferred learning style; (e) the caregiver's physical and emotional capacity to provide care (caregiver stress, fatigue, support system); (f) the home environment's adequacy for the child's safety and care needs. | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.2.6 | Assigned RN | Document the immunization status of the pediatric patient, comparing to the current CDC childhood immunization schedule. If immunizations are not current, notify the physician and provide a referral recommendation per the plan of care. | At the SOC visit. |\n| 6.2.7 | Assigned RN | Document the SOC assessment using the Pediatric Assessment Template (Appendix A) within 24 hours of the SOC visit. | Within 24 hours. |\n\n---",
