        id: "12-suicidality-assessment-and-safety-planning",
        title: "Suicidality Assessment and Safety Planning",
        level: 3,
        order: 12,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.5.1 | Assigned RN | When a patient endorses PHQ-9 item 9 (thoughts of being better off dead or hurting yourself) with a score ≥1, immediately conduct a structured suicidality assessment using the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS) or equivalent validated tool, assessing: (a) the frequency, intensity, and duration of suicidal ideation; (b) the presence of a plan; (c) the presence of means (access to firearms, medications, or other methods); (d) history of prior suicide attempts; (e) current protective factors (reasons for living, social support, religious beliefs). | Immediately upon PHQ-9 item 9 endorsement. |\n| 6.5.2 | Assigned RN | Based on the suicidality assessment, classify the risk level: (a) Low risk — passive death wish without intent or plan; no history of attempts; strong protective factors: Notify physician within 24 hours; MSW referral; increase visit frequency; safety education; document safety plan with patient; (b) Moderate risk — active suicidal ideation without specific plan; or ideation with plan but no immediate means: Notify physician within 4 hours; MSW contact same day; physician may order crisis intervention or psychiatric evaluation; document safety plan; (c) High risk — active suicidal ideation with plan and means; active intent: Call 911 immediately; do not leave the patient alone; notify physician immediately; complete incident report per RM-ER-002; stay with the patient until emergency services arrive. | Immediately upon completion of suicidality assessment. |\n| 6.5.3 | Assigned RN | For patients at Low or Moderate risk, develop a written safety plan with the patient, documenting: (a) warning signs that suicidal thoughts are intensifying; (b) internal coping strategies (what the patient can do independently to manage the thoughts); (c) social contacts the patient can reach for distraction (non-crisis); (d) family members or friends who can provide support; (e) crisis resources — National Suicide Prevention Lifeline (988), Crisis Text Line (Text HOME to 741741), local crisis center phone number; (f) means restriction — discussion with the patient and caregiver about securing access to potential means (medications locked away, firearms secured). | At the visit where the suicidality assessment is completed. |\n| 6.5.4 | Director of Nursing | The Director of Nursing shall be notified within 2 hours of any suicidality finding, regardless of risk level. The Director of Nursing shall ensure that appropriate clinical and administrative actions are completed, that the Governing Body is informed at the next quarterly meeting through the Administrator, and that the incident is reviewed in QAPI per QA-AE-001. | Within 2 hours of the finding. |\n\n---",
