        id: "11-symptom-management-at-end-of-life",
        title: "Symptom Management at End of Life",
        level: 3,
        order: 11,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.4.1 | Assigned RN | Assess and manage the following common end-of-life symptoms at each visit, using physician-ordered interventions: (a) Pain — per CL-SD-014; for patients transitioning to comfort care, pain management shall be prioritized over concerns about opioid adverse effects; titration to comfort is the goal. (b) Dyspnea — assess using the Modified Borg Dyspnea Scale or numeric rating; manage with physician-ordered opioids (opioids are the most effective pharmacologic treatment for dyspnea at end of life and shall not be withheld out of respiratory depression concern in patients who are comfort-focused); supplemental oxygen if beneficial; positioning (head of bed elevated, fan directed toward face); pursed-lip breathing if tolerated. (c) Nausea and vomiting — assess frequency and triggers; administer antiemetics per physician order; address reversible causes (constipation, medications). (d) Constipation — a universal side effect of opioid therapy; assess bowel function at every visit; ensure a bowel regimen is in place for all patients on opioids; contact physician if no bowel movement in >3 days. (e) Agitation and delirium — assess using the Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) or clinical observation; identify and manage reversible causes (urinary retention, constipation, pain, medications); administer physician-ordered antipsychotics or benzodiazepines for terminal agitation; maintain a calm, quiet environment; keep family informed. (f) Oral secretions (death rattle) — position the patient laterally; administer physician-ordered anticholinergic medications; educate family that the sound is not distressing to the patient. (g) Mouth care — provide or teach aggressive mouth care (every 2 hours) for patients with dry mouth or who are not eating or drinking. | At each visit for patients in the palliative/end-of-life phase. |\n| 6.4.2 | Assigned RN | Document each symptom assessment with a validated score or objective observation, the intervention provided, and the patient's response following the intervention. | Within 24 hours of each visit. |\n| 6.4.3 | Assigned RN | Contact the physician within 4 hours when any end-of-life symptom is not controlled by the current medication regimen, to obtain orders for dose adjustment, additional medications, or alternative interventions. | Within 4 hours of identifying uncontrolled symptoms. |\n\n---",
        scormChunkHint: "lesson",
