        id: "12-hospice-referral-coordination",
        title: "Hospice Referral Coordination",
        level: 3,
        order: 12,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.5.1 | Assigned RN / MSW | When hospice referral is clinically indicated or patient-requested, initiate the hospice conversation as a collaborative process: (a) Ensure the patient and family understand what hospice is — a Medicare-covered benefit providing comfort-focused care at home, including 24-hour nursing support, medications for the terminal diagnosis, durable medical equipment, social work, chaplaincy, and bereavement support; (b) Ensure the patient and family understand what hospice is not — it is not giving up, it is not hastening death, and the patient may revoke hospice at any time; (c) Ensure the patient and family understand that electing hospice means changing the focus from curative to comfort care, which typically involves stopping curative treatments for the terminal diagnosis; (d) Answer questions honestly and compassionately, using plain language. | When clinically indicated or patient-requested. |\n| 6.5.2 | Assigned RN | Notify the attending physician that hospice referral is being considered, provide a clinical summary of the patient's trajectory, and request the physician's input and, if appropriate, certification of a terminal prognosis of 6 months or less. | Within 24 hours of the hospice conversation initiation. |\n| 6.5.3 | MSW | Identify hospice agencies that serve the patient's geographic area and accept the patient's payer source (Medicare, Medi-Cal, or commercial insurance). Present options to the patient and family, respecting their right to select the hospice provider of their choice. Facilitate the referral per CL-CP-007, Section 6.4. | Within 48 hours of the patient's decision to pursue hospice. |\n| 6.5.4 | Assigned RN | Coordinate the clinical transition: (a) prepare the clinical transfer summary per CL-CP-007 Appendix A; (b) conduct a warm handoff with the hospice admitting nurse; (c) transmit the complete medication list, advance directive documentation, and relevant clinical records to the hospice agency; (d) complete the discharge OASIS for the home health episode. | Before or on the date of hospice election. |\n| 6.5.5 | Assigned RN | Ensure that the patient and family have been informed that: (a) home health and hospice cannot be provided simultaneously for the same terminal diagnosis under Medicare (the patient must elect one or the other); (b) the patient may revoke hospice at any time; (c) the agency's staff will remain available for questions until the hospice agency assumes care. | At the time of the transition discussion. |\n\n---",
