        body: "| Step | Action | Responsible Party | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.3.1 | The Compliance Officer shall initiate a reasonable diligence investigation within 3 business days of logging. The investigation shall include: (a) identification of all claims and episodes potentially affected; (b) review of clinical documentation, billing records, coding worksheets, and remittance advice for each affected claim; (c) determination of whether the overpayment resulted from a single isolated error, a recurring pattern, or systemic failure; (d) identification of root cause; (e) quantification of the total overpayment amount. | Compliance Officer (lead); Revenue Cycle Director (billing); Director of Nursing (clinical); Certified Coder (coding) | Investigation initiated within 3 business days of logging |\n| 6.3.2 | For isolated single-claim overpayments (e.g., duplicate payment, obvious coding error on one claim), the investigation and quantification shall be completed within 14 calendar days. | Revenue Cycle Director + Compliance Officer | Within 14 calendar days |\n| 6.3.3 | For multi-claim or potentially systemic overpayments, the Compliance Officer shall determine whether statistical sampling and extrapolation is appropriate. If the potential overpayment involves more than 10 claims with similar error characteristics, the Compliance Officer shall engage a qualified statistical consultant or use CMS/OIG-accepted sampling methodology per RAT-STATS or equivalent. | Compliance Officer | Determination within 14 calendar days; sampling plan developed within 21 calendar days |\n| 6.3.4 | The investigation shall determine the lookback period — the timeframe during which the error may have occurred. Per 42 CFR § 401.305(f), the agency must look back 6 years from the date the overpayment was received. The Compliance Officer shall apply the lookback period appropriate to the identified error pattern. | Compliance Officer | As part of investigation |\n| 6.3.5 | All investigation activities, findings, calculations, and supporting documentation shall be maintained in the Overpayment Investigation File (Appendix C). The file shall be treated as confidential and may be subject to attorney-client privilege if legal counsel is engaged. | Compliance Officer | Ongoing throughout investigation |\n| 6.3.6 | The investigation and quantification shall be completed, and the refund submitted, within the 60-day deadline from the identification date. If full quantification is not possible within 60 days despite reasonable diligence, the agency shall: (a) refund the estimated amount within 60 days; (b) continue the investigation; (c) submit a supplemental refund for any additional amount identified. This approach is supported by CMS guidance but does not extend the 60-day deadline for the initial report and refund. | Compliance Officer + Revenue Cycle Director | Within 60 calendar days of identification date |\n| 6.3.7 | The Compliance Officer shall provide weekly status updates to the CFO and Administrator for all open overpayment investigations. Status updates shall include: investigation progress, current estimated amount, 60-day deadline status, and any barriers to completion. | Compliance Officer | Weekly |\n\n---",
