        id: "17-what-surveyors-and-auditors-will-look-for",
        title: "What Surveyors and Auditors Will Look For",
        level: 3,
        order: 17,
        body: "CMS surveyors conducting a standard survey under the State Operations Manual (SOM) Appendix B will specifically verify:\nEvidence that the agency holds a current, valid state license. Surveyors will request a copy of the current California home health agency license and verify it is not expired. They will also verify that the license is displayed in the agency's principal office. An expired or suspended license is a threshold finding that may terminate the survey process and trigger immediate CMS notification.\nEvidence that the agency's Medicare certification is in good standing. Surveyors will verify the CCN and cross-reference the agency's PECOS enrollment status. Any discrepancy between the agency's reported certification status and CMS records is a critical finding.\nEvidence that the agency responds to survey findings. Surveyors will review prior survey history and verify that Plans of Correction were submitted timely and that corrective actions were implemented and sustained.\nEvidence that the Governing Body monitors the agency's credential status. Surveyors will review Governing Body minutes for documented credential verification. Minutes that do not reference licensure or certification status reviews may result in a finding of inadequate governance oversight.\nEvidence that the agency reports required changes to CMS and the state. Surveyors will verify that changes in address, ownership, administrator, or services were reported as required. Unreported changes are a common deficiency.\nEvidence of accreditation currency (if the agency holds accreditation). If the agency relies on accreditation for deemed status, surveyors will verify that accreditation is current and that the accrediting organization's most recent survey results are on file.\n\n---",
