        level: 3,
        order: 18,
        body: "CMS surveyors and state agency reviewers assessing a closure or CHOW will verify:\nEvidence that patients were properly notified and transitioned. This is the primary regulatory concern. CMS will verify that all patients received advance written notice, were offered a choice of alternative providers, and were safely transitioned with appropriate clinical documentation (OASIS, clinical summaries, medication reconciliation). Patient abandonment — a patient left without arranged continued care — is the most serious potential finding.\nEvidence that CMS and state notifications were timely. CMS will review the timing of the agency's notification relative to the closure or CHOW effective date. Late or absent notifications are regulatory violations.\nEvidence that clinical records were preserved and accessible. CMS may request records for patients served during the agency's period of certification. Inability to produce records is a post-closure compliance failure.\nEvidence that OASIS submissions were completed. CMS will verify that all required OASIS assessments (transfer, discharge) were completed and transmitted per OASIS requirements.\nEvidence that financial obligations were resolved. CMS may conduct a post-closure audit of claims submitted during the agency's period of certification. Unresolved overpayments, late-filed claims, or billing irregularities are compliance concerns.\n\n---",
