        id: "17-what-surveyors-and-auditors-will-look-for",
        title: "What Surveyors and Auditors Will Look For",
        level: 3,
        order: 17,
        body: "CMS surveyors and OIG reviewers assessing the agency's compliance program under SOM Appendix B and OIG Compliance Program Guidance will specifically verify:\nEvidence that a formal conflict of interest policy exists and is implemented. Surveyors will request the policy and verify it applies to all Governing Body members and senior leadership, not just the board chair.\nEvidence of annual disclosure completion. Surveyors will request completed disclosure forms for all current Governing Body members and verify they are current. A disclosure form that is more than 12 months old without a renewal is a deficiency.\nEvidence that disclosures are reviewed and acted upon. Surveyors will look for Compliance Officer review memos and Governing Body action in meeting minutes. A stack of disclosure forms with no evidence of review is a compliance program deficiency.\nEvidence of recusal compliance. If surveyors identify a disclosed conflict, they will cross-reference meeting minutes to verify the individual did not participate in discussion or vote on the conflicted matter. Meeting minutes that do not document recusals are a common deficiency.\nEvidence of conflict management plans for material conflicts. Surveyors will look for written plans defining how active conflicts are monitored. No plan means no management — surveyors treat this as a governance failure.\nEvidence of a referral relationship review. OIG reviewers in particular will assess whether the agency has examined its Governing Body members' and senior leaders' relationships with referral sources and vendors for Anti-Kickback Statute risk.\nConfidentiality and access controls on disclosure records. Surveyors will verify that disclosure forms are maintained in a restricted access file, not in general operational files visible to all staff.\n\n---",
