        id: "16-common-failure-points",
        title: "Common Failure Points",
        level: 3,
        order: 16,
        body: "| Failure Point | Risk | Mitigation |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| Self-assessment conducted but no written report or action plan produced. | Evidence of process without product; surveyor may view as perfunctory compliance exercise with no governance value. | Section 6.4 requires a finalized Summary Report and action plan within 35 days of Q1; filed in restricted governance file. |\n| All domains rated as satisfactory or excellent with no improvement areas identified. | Surveyor skepticism; implies the self-assessment was not conducted honestly or rigorously. | Governance Chair sets the expectation that the self-assessment is a genuine improvement tool; Compliance Officer frames distribution materials accordingly. |\n| Individual self-assessment forms not treated as privileged — filed in general governance file accessible to surveyors. | Members less likely to respond honestly if forms are not confidential; privacy risk. | Individual forms maintained in restricted privileged file by Compliance Officer; only aggregated findings filed in general governance file. |\n| Improvement action plan items never completed or tracked. | Self-assessment becomes a check-the-box exercise with no governance improvement value; same gaps recur annually. | Quarterly tracking built into standing agenda per Section 6.5; Chair escalates missed deadlines at next quarterly meeting. |\n| Self-assessment skipped in a given year without documentation. | Compliance gap; governance effectiveness cannot be demonstrated; prior year improvement gaps persist unaddressed. | If skipped, Compliance Officer documents in annual compliance report and presents gap to Governing Body per Section 6.6. |\n\n---",
