        id: "10-intake-screening-clinical-eligibility",
        title: "Intake Screening — Clinical Eligibility",
        level: 3,
        order: 10,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 5.3.1 | Intake RN / Clinical Manager | Perform clinical eligibility screening based on referral information. Evaluate: (a) homebound status indicators; (b) skilled nursing or therapy need; (c) medical necessity for home health services; (d) physician willingness to certify/order services; (e) patient/caregiver willingness to participate; (f) safety of the home environment (based on available information). | Within 4 hours (Urgent) / 24 hours (Routine). |\n| 5.3.2 | Intake RN / Clinical Manager | If clinical eligibility cannot be determined from referral documents alone, contact the referral source or physician office to obtain additional clinical information. Document all outreach attempts and outcomes. | Within 24 hours; minimum 2 attempts before escalation. |\n| 5.3.3 | Intake RN / Clinical Manager | Document the clinical eligibility determination on the Intake Clinical Screening Form (Appendix A) with rationale. Sign and date the form. | Upon completion of screening. |\n| 5.3.4 | Clinical Manager | Review and approve all clinical eligibility determinations for complex or borderline cases including: (a) patients with behavioral health diagnoses as primary condition; (b) patients with homebound status concerns; (c) patients requiring services the agency has limited experience delivering; (d) patients with active wound vac, TPN, or complex infusion needs. | Within 4 hours of escalation. |\n\n---",
