        id: "10-incident-classification",
        title: "Incident Classification",
        level: 3,
        order: 10,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 5.3.1 | Risk Manager | Classify the incident severity using the Incident Severity Classification Scale (Appendix C): Level 1 — No Harm/Near Miss: Event occurred but no injury or harm resulted. Level 2 — Minor Harm: Temporary, minor injury requiring minimal intervention (e.g., first aid). Level 3 — Moderate Harm: Injury requiring medical treatment or resulting in temporary impairment. Level 4 — Serious Harm: Significant injury requiring hospitalization, surgery, or resulting in permanent impairment. Level 5 — Sentinel Event: Death, or serious physical or psychological injury, or risk thereof. | Within 72 hours of report receipt. |\n| 5.3.2 | Risk Manager | Classify the incident by type: ☐ Patient fall ☐ Medication error ☐ Infection ☐ Equipment malfunction ☐ Staff injury ☐ Vehicle accident ☐ Verbal/physical threat ☐ Property damage ☐ HIPAA breach ☐ Documentation error ☐ Missed visit impacting care ☐ Other | Concurrent with severity classification. |\n| 5.3.3 | Risk Manager | Determine investigation tier: Level 1–2: Manager-level review and documentation. Level 3: Risk Manager investigation with written report. Level 4–5: Full investigation with root cause analysis (per QA-AE-002). | Concurrent with classification. |\n\n---",
