        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.5.1 | Exposed Employee | Report any potential ATD exposure incident to a supervisor and to the Director of Nursing immediately. An ATD exposure incident includes: (a) being in the same room for ≥15 minutes without appropriate respiratory protection with a person later confirmed to have an airborne ATD; (b) any unprotected AGP exposure. | Immediately upon recognition of exposure. |\n| 6.5.2 | Director of Nursing / Risk Manager | Initiate an ATD Exposure Investigation within 24 hours: (a) document the date, time, location, and nature of exposure; (b) identify the source patient or individual; (c) determine whether ATD status of exposure source is known or suspect; (d) assess whether the employee's PPE was correctly donned during exposure. | Investigation initiated within 24 hours. |\n| 6.5.3 | Risk Manager | Arrange post-exposure medical evaluation at the agency's designated occupational health provider at no cost to the employee. The evaluation must include: (a) assessment of exposure circumstances; (b) testing appropriate to the ATD (e.g., TB test, COVID test); (c) recommendations for prophylaxis where applicable (e.g., measles PEP); (d) work restriction assessment. | Post-exposure evaluation: within 24 hours for high-risk exposures; within 2 business days for moderate-risk. |\n| 6.5.4 | Risk Manager | Document the exposure investigation and response on the ATD Exposure Incident Form (Appendix D). File in the employee's medical file (separate from personnel file per CMIA requirements — CO-CA-001, HR-WM-007). | Within 5 business days of incident; retain for duration of employment + 30 years per 8 CCR §5199(h). |\n| 6.5.5 | Risk Manager | If the ATD exposure results in a work-related illness, record on the OSHA 300 Log (Form 300) per RM-OS-001 Section 6.5 and 8 CCR §14300. Report to Cal/OSHA if hospitalization or death results. | Recording: within 7 days of determination. Reporting: per RM-OS-001 thresholds. |\n| 6.5.6 | Risk Manager / Director of Nursing | Conduct a root cause analysis of each ATD exposure incident (per QA-AE-002) to determine whether an ECP deficiency contributed to the exposure. Update the ECP or training within 30 days of the RCA findings. | RCA completed within 14 days; ECP/training update within 30 days. |\n\n---",
