        title: "Engineering and Work Practice Controls",
        level: 3,
        order: 9,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.2.1 | Risk Manager / Director of Nursing | Evaluate and implement, to the extent feasible, engineering controls for all sharps procedures performed in patient homes: (a) all IV insertions shall use needleless or safety-engineered IV access systems; (b) all blood draws shall use safety-engineered phlebotomy devices; (c) finger-stick devices shall be self-retracting; (d) all agency-supplied sharps shall be safety-engineered as commercially available. | Prior to ECP effective date; update annually with sharps injury review and device availability. |\n| 6.2.2 | Risk Manager | Conduct an annual review of available safety-engineered sharps devices, taking into account: sharps injury log data, frontline employee input, and commercial availability. Document that safer alternatives were considered; if not implemented, document clinical or technical infeasibility. | Annually; documented in annual ECP review. |\n| 6.2.3 | All Clinical Staff | Perform all sharps procedures using work practice controls: (a) NEVER recap needles using a two-hand technique; (b) needles may be recapped using a one-hand scoop technique or a mechanical recapping device only when required for safe disposal transport; (c) needles shall never be bent, broken, or removed from disposable syringes by hand; (d) broken glassware shall be handled with mechanical device (forceps, brush), not bare hands; (e) blood or OPIM specimens shall be contained in leak-proof containers. | Every procedure. |\n| 6.2.4 | All Clinical Staff | Dispose of all contaminated sharps immediately after use in an agency-supplied, OSHA-compliant sharps disposal container (rigid, puncture-resistant, leak-proof, labeled with the biohazard symbol and the word \"BIOHAZARD\"). Field staff shall carry a portable sharps container to each visit. | Immediately after each use. |\n| 6.2.5 | All Clinical Staff | Wash hands immediately after removing gloves and after any skin contact with blood or OPIM. When handwashing is not immediately available in the field, use ABHR (alcohol-based hand rub) immediately and wash with soap and water as soon as feasible. | After each patient contact involving blood/OPIM. |\n| 6.2.6 | All Clinical Staff | Perform mouth-to-mouth resuscitation using a disposable CPR barrier device (pocket mask or face shield) from the agency-issued field supply kit. Never perform unprotected mouth-to-mouth resuscitation. | Whenever CPR is performed. |\n\n---",
