        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.6.1 | Exposed Employee | For any exposure incident (needlestick, splash to eye/mucous membrane, broken skin contact with blood/OPIM): (a) immediately wash the affected skin area thoroughly with soap and water; (b) for eye/mucous membrane exposure, flush with clean water for ≥15 minutes; (c) do NOT apply caustic agents, bleach, or anti-infective agents to the wound; (d) report to supervisor and Director of Nursing immediately. | Immediately upon exposure. |\n| 6.6.2 | Supervisor / Director of Nursing | Upon receipt of exposure report: (a) confirm initial first aid was performed; (b) contact the agency's designated PLHCP/occupational health provider immediately to arrange same-day post-exposure evaluation; (c) document the exposure circumstances on the BBP Exposure Incident Form (Appendix C). | Within 2 hours of exposure report. |\n| 6.6.3 | Risk Manager | Arrange and authorize the employee's post-exposure evaluation at no cost. The evaluation must include: (a) documentation of the route and circumstances of exposure; (b) identification of the source patient, if possible, with source patient testing (with consent per California law — CMIA requirements apply); (c) HIV baseline testing of exposed employee; (d) HBV and HCV baseline testing; (e) assessment for HIV PEP if indicated (must commence within 72 hours); (f) PLHCP written opinion within 15 days of evaluation. | Same-day evaluation arranged by supervisor. PEP assessment within 2–4 hours if HIV source risk. |\n| 6.6.4 | Risk Manager | If the source patient is known or suspected to have HIV: ensure the employee is informed of the availability of HIV PEP and that PEP must begin within 72 hours for maximum effectiveness. Ensure the occupational health provider assesses PEP eligibility. | Within 2 hours of determining source HIV status. |\n| 6.6.5 | Risk Manager | Receive the PLHCP written opinion within 15 business days of the evaluation and provide a copy to the employee. The opinion shall state only: (a) whether HBV vaccine is indicated and was provided; (b) that the employee was informed of results and any necessary follow-up. The written opinion shall NOT include the employee's diagnosis, specific HIV/HBV/HCV test results, or other medical information. | Within 15 days of evaluation; provide copy to employee. |\n| 6.6.6 | Risk Manager | Document the exposure incident in the Sharps Injury Log (Appendix D) if it involved a sharp device, per 8 CCR §5193(h)(4). Record: type and brand of device, department/work area (patient home visit), and explanation of incident. Log shall be de-identified before any aggregate review by personnel other than the Responsible Person. | Within 7 days of incident. |\n| 6.6.7 | Risk Manager | Record any work-related BBP illness on the OSHA 300 Log per RM-OS-001. Report to Cal/OSHA immediately if the exposure results in hospitalization or death. | Per RM-OS-001 recordkeeping requirements. |\n| 6.6.8 | Risk Manager / Director of Nursing | Conduct a post-exposure Root Cause Analysis per QA-AE-002 to determine contributing factors, including whether engineering controls were in place and used, whether PPE was available and used, and whether a safer device could have prevented the exposure. Update ECP and device selection within 30 days of RCA completion. | RCA within 14 days; ECP update within 30 days. |\n\n---",
