        level: 3,
        order: 12,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 6.5.1 | Recognizing Employee or Callers | Recognize heat illness emergency warning signs: (a) loss of consciousness or unresponsiveness; (b) confusion, disorientation, slurred speech; (c) hot, dry, or flushed skin (cessation of sweating in extreme heat); (d) seizure. For ANY of these signs — CALL 911 IMMEDIATELY. Do not delay calling 911 to contact supervisor first. Move employee to shade/cool area while awaiting EMS. | Immediately upon recognizing emergency heat illness signs. |\n| 6.5.2 | Responding Employee | While awaiting EMS: (a) move the affected employee to shade or cooler environment; (b) remove outer clothing if possible; (c) apply cool (not ice-cold) water to skin — wrists, armpits, neck; (d) fan the employee; (e) have employee sip cool water if conscious and able to swallow — do NOT give water to unconscious/confused persons; (f) stay with the employee until EMS arrives. | While awaiting EMS. |\n| 6.5.3 | Supervisor | After being notified of a heat illness emergency: (a) confirm 911 was called; (b) notify Risk Manager and Administrator immediately; (c) contact the patient household (if incident occurred in patient home) to provide information as needed; (d) document all circumstances in the Heat Illness Incident Form (Appendix C). | Immediately upon notification. |\n| 6.5.4 | Risk Manager | Following any employee heat illness incident: (a) initiate an incident investigation per RM-OS-001 Section 6.5; (b) conduct a root cause analysis per QA-AE-002; (c) identify whether program failures contributed; (d) update training or procedures within 30 days of RCA completion. | Investigation within 24 hours; RCA within 14 days; program update within 30 days. |\n\n---",
