        title: "Exposure Incident Response",
        level: 3,
        order: 12,
        body: "| Step | Responsible Party | Action | Timeframe |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 5.5.1 | Exposed Employee | Immediately following an exposure incident: (a) wash the affected area with soap and water (needlestick: allow to bleed, then wash; splash: flush eyes/mucous membranes with water for 15 minutes); (b) notify the supervisor. | Immediately. |\n| 5.5.2 | Supervisor | Direct the employee to seek post-exposure medical evaluation from a designated healthcare provider or emergency department. | Within 2 hours. |\n| 5.5.3 | Exposed Employee | Complete an Incident Report per RM-ER-002. | Within 24 hours. |\n| 5.5.4 | Risk Manager | Ensure post-exposure evaluation and follow-up per OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard (29 CFR § 1910.1030(f)) including: (a) documentation of route and circumstances of exposure; (b) identification of source individual (if possible and with consent); (c) blood testing of exposed employee (with consent); (d) post-exposure prophylaxis as recommended by healthcare provider; (e) counseling. | Within 24 hours; follow-up per medical provider. |\n| 5.5.5 | Risk Manager | Investigate the incident to determine if controls or work practices can be improved to prevent recurrence. | Within 14 calendar days. |\n| 5.5.6 | Risk Manager | Maintain the Sharps Injury Log (Appendix B) as required by OSHA, recording: (a) type and brand of device; (b) department/work area; (c) description of incident. Employee identity is kept confidential. | Within 14 days of incident; log retained 5 years. |\n\n---",
